/* Original CAT learning and practice material. Not copied from coaching providers. */
const extraLessons=[
['averages','qa','Averages & weighted averages','Find the balance behind a group.','The arithmetic mean is total divided by count. When groups combine, recover their totals before taking a new mean. A weighted average must lie between the smallest and largest component values.','New mean = (old mean × old count + sum of additions) / new count. Deviation method: choose a convenient reference, average deviations, then add the reference back.','Ten students average 60; five others average 72. Combined average = (600+360)/15 = 64.','A change in group size changes the weight; never average averages blindly.'],
['equations','qa','Linear systems & word problems','Name your unknowns, then translate.','Use independent relationships to form equations. In age, price and digit problems, distinguish current values from values after a change. Check that the solution satisfies the story, not just the equations.','For ax+by=c and dx+ey=f, elimination removes one variable by matching its coefficients. A two-digit number with tens digit a and units digit b is 10a+b.','If x+y=10 and 2x+y=14, subtract the first equation from the second: x=4; then y=6.','Two equations can describe the same information. Count independent conditions.'],
['inequalities','qa','Inequalities & absolute values','Reason about ranges, not just points.','Solve polynomial inequalities by locating roots, splitting the number line into intervals and checking signs. Absolute value measures distance from zero.','|x−a|<b means a−b<x<a+b for b>0. For real a,b: a²+b²≥2ab. Multiplying by a negative number reverses the inequality.','|x−3|<2 gives 1<x<5. For (x−2)(x−5)>0, the solution is x<2 or x>5.','Denominators cannot equal zero; roots of a denominator are excluded even in non-strict inequalities.'],
['functions','qa','Functions, graphs & domains','Understand what goes in and what comes out.','The domain is the allowed input set; range is the set of outputs attained. For compositions, the inner output must be a valid input to the outer function. Identify intercepts and symmetry before plotting.','(f∘g)(x)=f(g(x)). A square-root expression requires a nonnegative radicand; a denominator must be nonzero.','For f(x)=√(x−2), the real domain is x≥2 and range is y≥0.','The domain of a composition can be smaller than the domains of the individual functions.'],
['logs','qa','Exponents, surds & logarithms','Rewrite quantities on a common scale.','A logarithm answers an exponent question. For real logarithms the argument must be positive, the base positive and not 1. Simplify powers with matching bases before calculating.','logₐ(xy)=logₐx+logₐy; logₐ(x/y)=logₐx−logₐy; logₐ(x^k)=k logₐx. For 0<a<1, logₐx decreases as x increases.','log₂32=5 because 2⁵=32. If 3^(x+1)=81=3⁴, x=3.','log(x+y) is not log x+log y. Check domains before applying identities.'],
['sequences','qa','Sequences & progressions','Spot the rule and sum efficiently.','An arithmetic progression has constant difference; a geometric progression has constant ratio. A sum formula is only valid after you identify the correct structure.','AP: aₙ=a+(n−1)d, Sₙ=n[2a+(n−1)d]/2. GP: aₙ=ar^(n−1), Sₙ=a(rⁿ−1)/(r−1) for r≠1.','The first ten odd integers form an AP. Their sum is 10(1+19)/2=100.','A sequence with a visually familiar start may follow a different rule; use the stated definition.'],
['remainders','qa','Remainders & cyclicity','Use repetition to tame large powers.','Reduce intermediate products modulo the divisor. Last-digit patterns cycle, so map the exponent to its position in the cycle. Check whether a cycle starts at exponent zero or one.','If a≡b (mod m), then a+c≡b+c and ac≡bc (mod m). Remainders on division by m lie from 0 to m−1.','Powers of 7 end in 7,9,3,1. Since 23 leaves remainder 3 on division by 4, 7²³ ends in 3.','A remainder of zero in the exponent cycle means the last position of the cycle, not the first.'],
['triangles','qa','Triangles & similarity','Find the shape inside the shape.','Use congruence and similarity to connect lengths. An angle bisector divides the opposite side in the ratio of adjacent sides. A median joins a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.','Heron: area=√[s(s−a)(s−b)(s−c)], s=(a+b+c)/2. Similar triangles with side ratio k have area ratio k².','If similar triangles have corresponding sides 3:5, their areas are 9:25.','Equal area does not imply congruence or similarity.'],
['circles','qa','Circles, tangents & quadrilaterals','Let angle relationships do the work.','The radius to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent. Tangent segments drawn from the same external point have equal lengths. Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary.','Circumference=2πr; sector area=(θ/360)πr². For a cyclic quadrilateral A+C=180°.','A cyclic quadrilateral has one angle 110°. Its opposite angle is 70°.','A line touching a circle in a sketch is not a tangent unless specified or proved.'],
['coordinate','qa','Coordinate geometry & lines','Move geometry onto a grid.','Choose convenient coordinates without assuming additional symmetry. Slopes connect line direction; distances use Pythagoras. Vertical lines have undefined slope.','Distance=√[(x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²]. Midpoint=((x₁+x₂)/2,(y₁+y₂)/2). A line can be written ax+by+c=0.','Distance from (1,2) to (4,6) is √(3²+4²)=5.','Perpendicular nonvertical, nonhorizontal lines have slopes whose product is −1. Handle horizontal and vertical lines separately.'],
['mensuration','qa','Solids & mensuration','Separate surface from volume.','Identify which faces are exposed when calculating surface area. Volume scales with the cube of a uniform length factor, while area scales with its square.','Cylinder: V=πr²h, curved surface=2πrh. Cone: V=πr²h/3. Sphere: V=4πr³/3, surface=4πr².','Doubling every dimension of a cylinder multiplies volume by 8, not by 2.','Use consistent units. A square-centimetre measurement cannot be added to cubic centimetres.'],
['probability','qa','Probability & conditional reasoning','Count the right sample space.','Conditional probability restricts attention to outcomes that meet the condition. Independent events do not change each other’s probability; mutually exclusive events cannot occur together.','P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B), P(B)>0. For independent A and B, P(A∩B)=P(A)P(B). At least one=1−P(none).','For two independent fair coin tosses, P(at least one head)=1−(1/2)²=3/4.','Mutual exclusivity and independence are different concepts.'],
['tone','varc','Author’s tone, purpose & structure','Notice what the writer is doing.','Separate subject matter from attitude. A passage about conflict can be neutral and analytical. Purpose asks why it was written; structure asks how its argument develops.','Use modest tone labels supported by wording: analytical, skeptical, qualified, critical, conciliatory. Track contrast and concession signals.','“The policy has potential, though evidence remains limited” expresses cautious optimism rather than outright rejection.','Do not infer an extreme tone from a single vivid word when the overall passage is balanced.'],
['completion','varc','Sentence placement & completion','Fit the bridge, not just the vocabulary.','A missing sentence must connect smoothly to both the sentence before it and the sentence after it. Track pronouns, time sequence, contrast and shifts in scope.','Read before + candidate + after. Check backward reference and forward continuity, then test the paragraph’s main argument.','A sentence beginning “This limitation” should follow a specific limitation, not merely introduce a topic.','A topical match can still fail logically at the insertion point.'],
['strengthen','varc','Strengthen, weaken & assumptions','Test the link from evidence to claim.','Identify conclusion, evidence and the missing bridge. Strengtheners make the conclusion more credible; weakeners reduce support. Neither needs to prove the conclusion absolutely true or false.','Ask: Could the evidence be true while the conclusion is false? Alternative causes and selection bias often expose a weak link.','If sales rose after an ad campaign, evidence that the whole market rose equally weakens the claim that the ad alone caused the increase.','An option that attacks an irrelevant side detail may leave the argument intact.'],
['charts','dilr','Bar charts, line graphs & pie charts','Read the encoding before the number.','Check axis scales, legends and baselines. A line graph shows movement across ordered values; a pie chart partitions a whole. A stacked chart requires separating component and total values.','Pie sector angle=share×360°. Percentage-point difference=p₂−p₁. Relative percentage change=(p₂−p₁)/p₁×100.','A share changing from 20% to 25% rises by 5 percentage points and by 25% relative to its original share.','A truncated vertical axis can exaggerate apparent differences. Use values, not visual height alone.'],
['distribution','dilr','Distribution & allocation','Place items without losing constraints.','Translate minimum, maximum, distinct and exactly conditions separately. Start with the tightest capacities. List cases explicitly when a choice affects several later constraints.','For nonnegative integer allocations x₁+…+xₖ=n, use constraints to bound each variable before enumerating. Symmetry can collapse equivalent cases.','Five distinct books are split between two named people, each receiving at least one. There are 2⁵−2=30 allocations.','Identical items and distinct items produce different counting models.'],
['binary','dilr','Binary logic & truth conditions','Check entire statements consistently.','Represent truth values and test cases rather than trusting intuition. “If P then Q” is false only when P is true and Q is false. An exclusive either/or differs from an inclusive or.','P⇒Q has contrapositive not Q⇒not P. Its converse Q⇒P is not automatically true.','“If it rains, the match is cancelled” does not say that cancellation implies rain. Other causes may cancel the match.','A liar’s compound statement can be false without every clause being false.'],
['ranking','dilr','Ranking, ordering & comparisons','Turn relative clues into a partial order.','Represent taller-than or earlier-than relations as directed chains. Distinguish a unique total order from several orders satisfying the same partial information.','With no ties: rank from top + rank from bottom = total + 1. Use transitivity only when the comparison measures the same attribute.','A is 7th from the top and 12th from the bottom: total=7+12−1=18.','If ties are allowed, the standard ranking shortcut may fail.'],
['optimization','dilr','Maxima, minima & feasible cases','Find the boundary without breaking a rule.','Establish a bound from totals and capacity, then construct an example that achieves it. A theoretical bound is not an answer until feasibility is checked.','To maximize one group, minimize the allocation forced into every other group. For integer quantities, round bounds in the correct direction.','Among 50 people divided into 4 nonempty groups, the largest possible group has 47: put one person in each other group.','An attractive upper bound can be impossible because of a hidden overlap or capacity constraint.']
].map(([id,subject,title,desc,concept,formula,example,trap])=>({id,subject,title,desc,concept,formula,example,trap}));
lessons.push(...extraLessons);
function addMC(subject,topic,text,options,answer,explanation,meta={}){q(subject,topic,text,options.map(String),answer,explanation);Object.assign(Q[Q.length-1],{difficulty:'Foundation',...meta})}
function addTITA(topic,text,answer,explanation,meta={}){Q.push({id:Q.length+1,subject:'qa',topic,text,options:[],answer:String(answer),explanation,type:'tita',difficulty:'Intermediate',...meta})}
// Parameterized arithmetic questions use explicit, fully worked values, with checked unique options.
for(let i=1;i<=8;i++){
 const cost=400+i*100,rate=10+i*5,sp=cost*(100+rate)/100;
 addMC('qa','profit',`An article costs ₹${cost}. At what selling price will the seller earn a profit of ${rate}%?`,[sp-cost*.1,sp,sp+cost*.1,cost],1,`Profit = ${cost} × ${rate}/100 = ₹${sp-cost}. Selling price = ${cost} + ${sp-cost} = ₹${sp}.`);
 const m=i+2,n=i+4,total=(m+n)*60;
 addMC('qa','ratio',`Anushka splits ₹${total} between books and a test series in the ratio ${m}:${n}. How much goes to the test series?`,[m*60,n*60,total/2,total],1,`Total parts = ${m+n}. Each part = ${total}/${m+n} = ₹60. The test series receives ${n} × 60 = ₹${n*60}.`);
 const count=8+i,avg=40+i,newVal=avg+count+1;
 addTITA('averages',`The average of ${count} numbers is ${avg}. One more number, ${newVal}, is added. What is the new average?`,avg+1,`Old sum = ${count} × ${avg} = ${count*avg}. New sum = ${count*avg+newVal}; new count = ${count+1}. New mean = ${avg+1}.`);
 const a=i+3,b=2*a;
 addMC('qa','work',`A completes a job in ${a} days and B in ${b} days. At their constant individual rates, what fraction of the job do they complete together in one day?`,[`1/${a}`,`3/${2*a}`,`1/${3*a}`,`2/${3*a}`],1,`Combined rate = 1/${a} + 1/${b} = 3/${2*a} jobs/day. Fractions are not required to be in simplest terms.`);
 const x=i+2;
 addTITA('algebra',`If x + 1/x = ${x}, what is x² + 1/x²?`,x*x-2,`Square the given equality: x² + 2 + 1/x² = ${x*x}. Subtract 2 to get ${x*x-2}.`);
 const first=3+i,d=2+i,terms=10;
 const sum=terms*(2*first+(terms-1)*d)/2;
 addMC('qa','sequences',`An arithmetic progression has first term ${first} and common difference ${d}. What is the sum of its first 10 terms?`,[sum-10,sum,sum+10,10*(first+d)],1,`S₁₀ = 10[2×${first}+9×${d}]/2 = ${sum}. The tenth term is ${first+9*d}.`,{difficulty:'Intermediate'});
}
const extraQuant=[
['numbers','What is the HCF of 84 and 126?',['14','21','42','63'],2,'84=2²×3×7 and 126=2×3²×7. The shared prime powers give 2×3×7=42.'],
['remainders','What is the last digit of 7²³?',['1','3','7','9'],1,'Powers of 7 have last digits 7,9,3,1, repeating every 4 powers. Since 23 mod 4 is 3, use the third digit: 3.'],
['logs','If log₂(x−1)=3, what is x?',['7','8','9','10'],2,'x−1=2³=8, so x=9. The domain requires x>1, which is satisfied.'],
['functions','For f(x)=2x+1 and g(x)=x², what is f(g(3))?',['19','37','49','10'],0,'Evaluate the inner function first: g(3)=9. Then f(9)=2×9+1=19.'],
['inequalities','For real x, which interval satisfies |x−4|<3?',['−3<x<3','1<x<7','x<1 or x>7','1≤x≤7'],1,'The distance from x to 4 is less than 3, so 4−3<x<4+3. The endpoints are excluded.'],
['coordinate','Find the distance between (−1,2) and (2,6).',['3','4','5','7'],2,'The coordinate differences are 3 and 4. Distance=√(3²+4²)=5.'],
['triangles','The sides of a triangle are 13, 14 and 15. Find its area.',['72','84','90','96'],1,'Semiperimeter s=21. Heron’s formula gives √(21×8×7×6)=√7056=84.'],
['circles','An angle of a cyclic quadrilateral is 118°. What is the opposite angle?',['62°','72°','118°','124°'],0,'Opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180°. Thus 180−118=62°.'],
['mensuration','Every linear dimension of a cylinder is multiplied by 3. Its volume is multiplied by:',['3','6','9','27'],3,'Volume=πr²h. Scaling r and h by 3 gives a factor of 3²×3=27.'],
['probability','Two fair coins are tossed independently. What is the probability of at least one head?',['1/4','1/2','3/4','1'],2,'The complement is two tails, with probability 1/4. Required probability=1−1/4=3/4.'],
['equations','If 2x+3y=19 and x+y=7, what is y?',['2','3','4','5'],3,'Double x+y=7 to obtain 2x+2y=14. Subtract from 2x+3y=19: y=5.'],
['averages','A class of 20 has average score 60; a class of 30 has average score 70. Find the combined average.',['64','65','66','68'],2,'Combined total=20×60+30×70=3300. Divide by 50 students to get 66.'],
['probability','A bag contains 3 red and 2 blue balls. Two are drawn without replacement. What is the probability both are red?',['3/10','2/5','9/25','1/5'],0,'P(first red)=3/5; then P(second red)=2/4. Product=6/20=3/10.'],
['speed','A 150-metre train passes a pole in 10 seconds. What is its speed in km/h?',['36','45','54','60'],2,'Speed=150/10=15 m/s. Multiply by 18/5 to convert to km/h: 54.'],
['modern','There are 30 students: 18 play chess, 16 play badminton and 8 play both. How many play neither?',['2','4','6','8'],1,'Union=18+16−8=26; neither=30−26=4.'],
['percentages','A salary rises from ₹40,000 to ₹46,000. What is the percentage increase?',['12%','13%','15%','18%'],2,'Increase=₹6000. Relative to the original ₹40000, it is 6000/40000×100=15%.']
];extraQuant.forEach(([t,txt,o,a,e])=>addMC('qa',t,txt,o,a,e,{difficulty:'Intermediate'}));
// Eight original reading passages, with four linked questions each.
const passages=[
{title:'The city and its shade',text:'When cities count newly planted trees, they capture an event rather than an outcome. A sapling on an exposed pavement may survive its first photograph and perish in its first summer. The difference between planting and growing is unglamorous: soil, water, protection and years of patient maintenance. None of this means that planting targets are useless. Targets can mobilise resources and draw attention to neglected neighbourhoods. But a target becomes misleading when it rewards the beginning of a process as if it were its successful completion. A more useful measure would ask whether residents actually experience shade several years later, and whether that benefit reaches areas that previously lacked it.',items:[
['What is the central argument?',['Tree planting should be abandoned.','Success should be measured by sustained benefits, not planting counts alone.','Every neighbourhood needs an identical number of trees.','Public photographs are harmful to trees.'],1,'The passage distinguishes the act of planting from long-term outcomes, while explicitly retaining a role for targets.'],
['Why does the author mention “soil, water, protection”?',['To list the hidden work required for trees to survive.','To prove saplings cannot grow on pavements.','To reject neighbourhood planning.','To describe an unrelated farming method.'],0,'The list illustrates maintenance: the sustained work obscured by headline planting numbers.'],
['Which inference is supported?',['A city with the most saplings always has the best tree policy.','Counting an easy-to-observe input can conceal unequal outcomes.','Shade is the only possible benefit of trees.','Targets never influence resource allocation.'],1,'The passage warns that counts can hide whether benefits persist and reach previously underserved areas.'],
['The author’s attitude toward planting targets is:',['Unconditionally enthusiastic','Qualified rather than wholly dismissive','Hostile to every form of measurement','Nostalgic for cities without trees'],1,'Targets can mobilise resources, but must not be confused with final outcomes. This is a qualified evaluation.']]},
{title:'The patient experiment',text:'An experiment that fails to support a prediction is often described as a dead end. That description confuses the researcher’s original hope with the value of the result. A well-designed study can narrow the set of plausible explanations even when its favoured explanation loses support. The qualification matters: an inconclusive result caused by unreliable measurement does not automatically advance knowledge. What makes a negative result useful is not disappointment itself, but the disciplined way in which alternatives have been tested. Scientific progress is therefore better understood as improving the map of uncertainty than as accumulating a shelf of agreeable answers.',items:[
['Which title best captures the passage?',['Why every failure is useful','Progress through disciplined uncertainty','The end of scientific experiments','Why predictions should be avoided'],1,'The main claim is that carefully designed negative results refine uncertainty, not that all failures help.'],
['Which result would the author regard as most useful?',['A preferred theory confirmed by an unreliable instrument.','A carefully controlled study ruling out one plausible explanation.','A study with measurements too noisy to interpret.','An agreeable answer without a test.'],1,'Controlled elimination of an alternative improves the map of uncertainty. Reliability is essential.'],
['What role does the qualification about measurement play?',['It limits the claim that negative results can advance knowledge.','It proves no experiment can be trusted.','It changes the topic to industrial design.','It claims disappointment improves instruments.'],0,'The qualification distinguishes informative negative evidence from mere inconclusiveness.'],
['The author would most likely disagree that:',['Evidence can narrow possible explanations.','Uncertainty can be mapped more accurately.','Only results agreeing with a prediction contribute to progress.','Design quality affects the value of a study.'],2,'The passage directly argues that a rigorous result can help even when the favoured prediction fails.']]},
{title:'The convenience of recommendations',text:'A recommendation system saves a reader from searching an immense catalogue, but convenience is never entirely neutral. By predicting what a person will enjoy from what they enjoyed before, it can make yesterday’s preferences a quiet boundary around tomorrow’s choices. This is not an argument for random recommendations: irrelevant suggestions quickly become noise. The design problem is to make room for meaningful surprise without abandoning relevance. A good system might occasionally offer something unfamiliar and explain the connection that makes it worth trying. Such a system treats a reader’s taste as capable of development rather than as a fixed label.',items:[
['The passage advocates recommendation systems that:',['Show only random items.','Balance relevance with opportunities to discover unfamiliar material.','Eliminate all personalisation.','Always repeat past choices exactly.'],1,'The author seeks meaningful surprise alongside relevance, not randomness or repetition.'],
['“A quiet boundary” refers to:',['A physical library wall.','The way past preferences may constrain future exposure.','A legal rule about reading.','The end of a catalogue.'],1,'The metaphor describes a narrowing of future choices based on historical behaviour.'],
['Which assumption underlies the proposed design?',['Readers’ interests can evolve.','Every unfamiliar book is enjoyable.','Explanations never influence choices.','Past preferences contain no useful information.'],0,'The proposal explicitly treats taste as capable of development, making evolution of interests central.'],
['Which suggestion most closely fits the passage?',['Recommend an unfamiliar topic with an explained link to a known interest.','Remove every item the reader might recognise.','Use popularity as the sole criterion.','Never show a new author.'],0,'An explained connection provides relevance while preserving room for discovery.']]},
{title:'The meaning of a price',text:'A low price can signal efficiency, but it can also conceal a cost paid elsewhere. A cheap delivery may depend on road space, worker time and packaging waste that do not appear fully on the customer’s bill. It would be equally careless to assume that a higher price proves social responsibility: an expensive service may simply have a larger margin. The useful question is not whether cheapness is virtuous or expensive goods are ethical. It is which costs have been counted, which have been shifted, and whether buyers have enough information to tell the difference. Price is a piece of evidence, not a complete moral account.',items:[
['What is the main point?',['Cheap products are always unethical.','Price alone does not reveal how fully costs are accounted for.','Expensive products are always sustainable.','Consumer choice is impossible.'],1,'Both low and high prices are ambiguous; the author asks about counted and shifted costs.'],
['Why mention a larger margin?',['To show that a high price may have explanations unrelated to responsibility.','To prove profits are illegal.','To praise every expensive service.','To argue packaging has no cost.'],0,'It supplies an alternative explanation for higher prices and blocks an easy moral inference.'],
['Which information would best help answer the author’s “useful question”?',['The colour of the delivery vehicle.','A transparent account of labour, waste and public-resource costs.','The celebrity endorsing the service.','A slogan describing the price as fair.'],1,'A cost account helps identify what is counted and what is shifted to others.'],
['The tone is best described as:',['Analytical and cautionary','Triumphant and celebratory','Mocking and personal','Indifferent and evasive'],0,'The passage distinguishes competing interpretations and cautions against simplistic judgement.']]},
{title:'Languages in motion',text:'When a language borrows a word, commentators sometimes describe the event as a loss of purity. Yet languages are not sealed containers; their histories include trade, migration and contact. Borrowing can provide names for new objects or new shades of meaning. This does not make every concern about language change foolish. When a dominant language displaces another from schools and public life, the loss of opportunities to use the smaller language is real. But displacement and borrowing are different processes. Protecting the conditions in which a language can be spoken need not require freezing its vocabulary.',items:[
['The passage chiefly distinguishes:',['Spoken words from written words.','Vocabulary borrowing from institutional displacement.','Trade from all forms of migration.','Old languages from useful languages.'],1,'The author separates normal borrowing from loss of domains in which a language can be used.'],
['The author would likely support:',['Banning every borrowed word.','Maintaining education in a language while allowing its vocabulary to evolve.','Replacing all small languages in schools.','Treating every language concern as foolish.'],1,'This preserves conditions of use without freezing vocabulary: the distinction the author endorses.'],
['The reference to schools serves to:',['Illustrate a domain where language displacement has consequences.','Prove that schools never affect language.','Recommend a universal vocabulary test.','Explain the origin of every borrowed word.'],0,'Schools are an example of public institutions whose language choices affect opportunities for use.'],
['Which is a supported inference?',['A language can change its vocabulary without losing its social role.','Borrowing always causes extinction.','No dominant language affects another.','All vocabularies were once pure.'],0,'Vocabulary evolution and social displacement are distinct; change need not mean loss of use.']]},
{title:'Repair and ownership',text:'To own an object once implied some practical freedom to maintain it. Increasingly, devices combine physical parts with software that only the manufacturer can authorise others to change. Supporters of these restrictions cite safety and security, concerns that cannot simply be dismissed. Yet a restriction applied to every repair, regardless of its risk, may protect a business model more than a user. A sensible repair policy would distinguish operations that genuinely threaten safety from routine maintenance. Ownership becomes thinner when the purchaser bears the cost of the object but lacks reasonable control over its useful life.',items:[
['The author’s preferred policy would:',['Permit every modification without limits.','Differentiate repair restrictions according to genuine risk.','Ban software from devices.','Reserve all maintenance for manufacturers.'],1,'The author recognises safety concerns but argues for distinguishing risky from routine operations.'],
['“Ownership becomes thinner” means:',['Devices physically lose weight.','The practical control associated with ownership is reduced.','Prices always fall.','All purchasers stop using devices.'],1,'The metaphor points to diminished control over maintenance and useful life.'],
['Which evidence would weaken the criticism of a blanket restriction most directly?',['Most restricted repairs create a demonstrated serious safety risk that cannot be mitigated.','The manufacturer has an attractive logo.','Some users like the device colour.','A rival device is expensive.'],0,'Evidence that the restrictions match unavoidable serious risks would strengthen the safety rationale.'],
['The passage treats security arguments as:',['Irrelevant in every case.','Potentially valid but insufficient to justify every restriction automatically.','Proof that ownership is unnecessary.','Identical to routine maintenance.'],1,'Safety and security cannot be dismissed, yet each restriction still needs justification.']]},
{title:'Working with a map',text:'A map is useful because it leaves things out. A street map suppresses soil type; a geological map may ignore bus stops. The omission is not necessarily a defect, since each map answers a different question. Trouble begins when a representation designed for one task is treated as an all-purpose picture of reality. A ranking of universities, for instance, may compress research output, resources and reputation into a single order, but tell a particular student little about teaching in a specialised programme. The response should be to ask what a representation is for, not to demand a map with every possible detail.',items:[
['What links maps to university rankings in the argument?',['Both must include all available details.','Both simplify reality for particular purposes.','Both are always deceptive.','Both measure the same characteristics.'],1,'Rankings illustrate how a useful simplification can be misapplied outside its intended task.'],
['Which question best follows the author’s advice?',['What decision was this ranking designed to support?','Why does every ranking omit bus stops?','Which map contains the most colours?','Can a single ranking replace every other consideration?'],0,'The author asks users to inspect the purpose of a representation.'],
['Which claim is rejected?',['Omission may make a map useful.','Different users may need different representations.','Any useful representation must contain every possible detail.','Research output may not capture a student’s programme needs.'],2,'The passage explicitly rejects demanding every possible detail in one map.'],
['The example of the specialised programme shows that:',['Aggregate measures can miss an individual decision’s relevant dimensions.','Teaching quality never matters.','Research cannot be measured.','Every university ranking has the same methodology.'],0,'A broad ranking may not answer a programme-specific question important to one student.']]},
{title:'The pace of attention',text:'Multitasking is often praised as a way to fit more work into a day. But tasks that require sustained thought do not combine as neatly as errands on a route. Switching from one problem to another can require reconstructing the context of each, creating a cost that is easy to overlook because the worker remains visibly busy. Some interruptions are necessary, and routine tasks can sometimes overlap without much loss. The relevant distinction is therefore not between movement and idleness, but between activity that advances an important task and activity that repeatedly resets it.',items:[
['The author’s main concern is:',['All interruptions must be prohibited.','Visible busyness can hide the costs of switching between demanding tasks.','Routine tasks require no attention.','A longer workday always improves output.'],1,'The passage focuses on hidden reconstruction costs and distinguishes meaningful progress from activity.'],
['Which practice most closely follows the argument?',['Protect blocks of sustained thought while allowing necessary interruptions.','Switch projects every two minutes regardless of need.','Judge productivity only by visible movement.','Never combine any routine errands.'],0,'The recommendation respects both concentration costs and the author’s qualifications.'],
['What does “reconstructing the context” imply?',['Returning to a task can require mental effort before progress resumes.','The task physically disappears.','Workers forget every prior thought permanently.','Every interruption is equally costly.'],0,'Switching back involves re-establishing the relevant state of the problem.'],
['The distinction between thought and errands helps the author:',['Explain why different kinds of tasks may have different switching costs.','Prove errands are worthless.','Claim no task can be scheduled.','Recommend longer routes.'],0,'The analogy contrasts tasks that combine relatively easily with sustained reasoning tasks.']]}
];
